1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:03,940
The world's tallest wind
turbine is 365 meters tall.

2
00:00:03,960 --> 00:00:06,670
If its generator failed up
there, how would you get it down?

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:07,960
Schipkau, Germany.

4
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:09,560
Hub height, 300 meters.

5
00:00:09,580 --> 00:00:11,480
Blade tip, 365.

6
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:13,170
Taller than the Eiffel Tower.

7
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:14,400
Here's what caught my eye:

8
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:17,100
the rotor is 126 meters across.

9
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:19,660
Same as the Vestas V126 I service.

10
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:24,690
But its hub sits between 87 and
166 meters, depending on version.

11
00:00:24,730 --> 00:00:25,470
Why so tall?

12
00:00:25,490 --> 00:00:27,710
Winds higher up are stronger
and more consistent.

13
00:00:27,730 --> 00:00:31,470
The developer expects about twice the
energy of a turbine with the same rotor.

14
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:32,760
That's still a projection.

15
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:35,340
My first question as a
technician is different:

16
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:35,920
maintenance.

17
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:38,350
No crane today can lift
the nacelle that high.

18
00:00:38,390 --> 00:00:38,930
The fix:

19
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:40,090
a telescoping tower.

20
00:00:40,110 --> 00:00:42,590
The inner tower slides
down inside the lattice.

21
00:00:42,610 --> 00:00:44,070
The crane reaches the machine.

22
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:45,620
Then the tower goes back up.

23
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:46,630
The tower changes.

24
00:00:46,650 --> 00:00:47,550
The rule doesn't:

25
00:00:47,570 --> 00:00:48,990
never climb a tower alone.

26
00:00:49,010 --> 00:00:50,080
Always at least two.

27
00:00:50,300 --> 00:00:53,280
Full V126 specs at sonersoylu.com/en.

28
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:54,260
The Turbine Tech.

29
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:55,830
Follow for the next question.
